     \"explanation\": \"The 'ATAQUE DE DICIONÁRIO OFFLINE' (Offline Dictionary Attack) is a cybersecurity technique designed to crack passwords without real-time interaction with the target system. This method is highly effective against weak and simple passwords, as it systematically tries numerous combinations from a predefined list or algorithmically generated possibilities [Data: Entities (644)].\\n\\nIn the context of WPA2-PSK networks, the attack process involves obtaining the four-way handshake, which is exchanged when a device connects to the access point. This handshake contains a hash of the network's password. Attackers then use specialized software to 'brute-force' this hash by comparing it against a large database of common passwords, known password patterns, or custom-generated wordlists. If a match is found, the attacker successfully recovers the pre-shared key and gains unauthorized access to the Wi-Fi network [Data: Entities (644), (641); Relationships (998)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Impact of Password Strength on WPA2-PSK Security\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The effectiveness of an offline dictionary attack against a WPA2-PSK network is almost entirely dependent on the strength of the pre-shared key (password). Simple, short, or common passwords can be cracked relatively quickly using readily available tools and wordlists [Data: Entities (641), (644)].\\n\\nConversely, strong passwords that are long, complex, and include a mix of uppercase and lowercase letters, numbers, and symbols, significantly increase the difficulty and time required for an offline dictionary attack. Such robust password practices are essential for maintaining the security of WPA2-PSK networks and preventing unauthorized access. The description of WPA2-PSK explicitly states that its security is 'heavily reliant on the complexity and uniqueness of the chosen password' [Data: Entities (641); Relationships (991)].\"\n        },\n        {\n    